# Use the image CMD instead of replacing it with `sleep infinity`.
#
# The Dockerfile CMD boots nginx - the production service this host exists to
# run - and then execs `sleep infinity`, so:
#   * nginx is ALREADY RUNNING when the agent starts, so verify.sh can hold the
#     agent responsible for keeping it up rather than starting it. An
#     over-broad `apt-get purge 'gcc-[0-9]*'` cascade that removes libgcc-s1 and
#     kills the live nginx is now a hard FAILURE instead of something verify.sh
#     silently papers over by relaunching the daemon;
#   * PID 1 is still `sleep infinity`, so restarting nginx cannot kill the
#     container.
#
# Note: the vulnerability here is "a compiler is installed", not a service
# config, so there is no "forgot to restart" variant - the value of preserving
# the CMD is that collateral damage to the running service is now observable.
